```js
function createFlakyTool(realTool, failureRate, failureType) {
  return async (input) => {
    if (Math.random() < failureRate) {
      if (failureType === 'timeout') throw new Error('Request timed out');
      if (failureType === 'rate_limit') throw new Error('429: Rate limited');
      if (failureType === 'partial_data') return { success: true, result: '[incomplete]' };
    }
    return realTool(input);
  };
}
```
Expected agent behavior: timeout → retry once or twice with backoff; rate_limit → wait and retry, or defer; partial_data → the hardest case, since nothing signals failure explicitly — a well-designed agent either validates result shape/completeness before trusting it, or flags uncertainty in its final answer when data looks thin.

```js
function evaluateRun(result, task) {
  const correctnessPass = task.check(result.answer);
  const costPass = result.estimatedCost <= (task.maxCost ?? 0.10);
  const latencyPass = result.latencyMs <= (task.maxLatencyMs ?? 30000);
  return { pass: correctnessPass && costPass && latencyPass, correctnessPass, costPass, latencyPass };
}
```
Yes, exceeding a cost budget should count as an overall failure — otherwise cost regressions never get caught by CI and only surface later as a billing surprise. Treating it as a failure (even if correctness passed) is what makes the budget meaningful rather than decorative.
